text_mt:
Aurva said:
One shall bear a single continuer of the lineage, and the other sixty thousand;
O fortunate ladies, choose according to your desire for offspring!

Aṃśumat said:
"O sage, whatever bad conduct was committed by my uncles, forgive that;
for the virtuous are devoted to helping others, and their essence is forbearance.

Viṣṇu is the duties, Viṣṇu is the fruits; Viṣṇu is the actions, Viṣṇu is the enjoyer of fruits;
Viṣṇu is the effect, Viṣṇu is the instruments—there is nothing apart from him.

Thus ends the thirteenth chapter, named 'The Discourse on Dharma', in the first quarter of the first part of the glorious Bṛhannāradīya Purāṇa.

Sanaka said:
I shall tell the determination of the lunar days and the rule of expiation;
listen to that, O best of sages, by which accomplishment of ritual actions comes to be.

Givers of food, O best of sages, go while eating delicious [delicacies];
givers of water go happily, drinking excellent milk;
givers of buttermilk and givers of curds obtain their respective enjoyments;
and givers of ghee, givers of honey, and givers of milk, O foremost of the twice-born,

having reached the surface of the earth, they become immovable beings and the like.
Nārada said:
O Lord, an ocean of compassion, a doubt has arisen in my mind;

Jayadhvaja said:
Listen, O tiger among Brahmins, to what was done formerly by me;
because I remember my past lives, I know that which produces wonder in the hearers.

Nevertheless, all the gods, longing for birth in Bhārata,
worship the lord of gods, the flawless Nārāyaṇa;
the multitudes of deities headed by Brahmā constantly revere them.

Nārada said:
Everything has been explained by you, O sage who knows the true nature of reality;
now I wish to hear the characteristic nature of the yugas.

